{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. After his father's assassination in 1152 Raymond III became the count of Tripoli at the age of 12 and his mother Hodierna acted as his regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was a member of the House of Toulouse and was related to several other prominent nobles in the Holy Land. He was a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and was also related to the Princes of Antioch. *Reign* During his reign Raymond III was involved in several conflicts with neighboring states including the Zengid dynasty and the Fatimid Caliphate. He also played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a regent for King Baldwin IV on two separate occasions.\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III was appointed as the regent for King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. *Regency* Raymond III acted as regent until 1176 when Baldwin IV came of age. However Baldwin IV's condition worsened and Raymond III was again appointed as regent in 1184. During his second regency Raymond III was involved in a bitter power struggle with Guy of Lusignan who was the husband of Baldwin IV's sister Sibylla. Raymond III opposed Guy's claim to the throne and instead supported the claim of Raymond of Poitiers' son Bohemond III of Antioch's son Baldwin.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond IV of Tripoli. He was known for his military prowess and his diplomatic skills and played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states in the Holy Land. Raymond III's legacy was marked by his complex and oft